.TH "NIAC" "1" "Sep 2026" "NIAC" "NIAC Manual"

.SH NAME
niac-status - Query the status of a running NIAC simulation


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBniac status [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Query the status of a running NIAC simulation.

.PP
This command connects to a running NIAC simulation and retrieves
current status information including:
  - Simulation state (running/stopped)
  - Network interface in use
  - Configuration file path
  - Device count
  - Uptime
  - Packet statistics (RX/TX)
  - Active error injections

.PP
Exit codes:
  0 - Simulation is running
  1 - Simulation is not running (socket not found or connection refused)
  2 - Error occurred (socket error, parse error, etc.)


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--api\fP=""
	Daemon API address (default: https://127.0.0.1:8445, or NIAC_API_URL)

.PP
\fB--cacert\fP=""
	Daemon certificate to trust (default: the local daemon's own, when visible)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for status

.PP
\fB--insecure\fP[=false]
	Skip TLS verification, for a daemon whose certificate this host cannot see

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Output status as JSON


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--storage-keep\fP=500
	Run history records to keep, pruned oldest first on start (0 keeps every run)

.PP
\fB--storage-path\fP=""
	Path to NIAC run history database (default: ~/.niac/niac.db)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Check simulation status
  niac status

  # Output status as JSON
  niac status --json

  # Use a custom socket path
  niac status --socket /var/run/niac.sock

  # Use in scripts
  if niac status > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "NIAC is running"
  else
    echo "NIAC is not running"
  fi
.EE


